[{:db/id 17592186358936 :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The time it takes for a student to complete math homework\n- The number of completed whole hours spent jogging during a week, ignoring any partial hour\n- The number of candies in a jar" :question/worked-solution "A discrete random variable has finitely many possible values or values that can be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement I measures elapsed time. The prompt does not restrict that duration to integer values, so it is a continuous random variable.\n- Statement II explicitly counts completed whole jogging hours. If the elapsed jogging time is $t$ hours, the recorded value is $\\lfloor t\\rfloor$. During one week, $0\\le t\\le168$, so the possible recorded values are the integers $0,1,2,\\ldots,168$. This is a discrete random variable.\n- Statement III counts candies. Its possible values are nonnegative integers, so it is a discrete random variable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"\n\nMeasuring elapsed time in hours alone does not make it discrete: fractional hours are possible. The explicit instruction to count completed whole hours makes Statement II discrete. The source explanation used integer hour counts; this wording states that convention directly." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
